# box.env — per-box NETWORK identity. Single source of truth for the LAN IP/subnet.
#
# Copy to env\box.env and set the values for THIS box, then run scripts\install.ps1.
# install.ps1 reads this file and:
#   - composes LAGUNA_LAN_HOST + LAGUNA_LAN_IP into LAGUNA_SITE_ADDR and exports it plus
#     LAGUNA_LAN_CIDR as machine env vars so the Caddy service picks them up
#     (the Caddyfile uses {$LAGUNA_SITE_ADDR} / {$LAGUNA_LAN_CIDR} placeholders),
#   - derives the Windows Firewall rule's allowed remote range from LAGUNA_LAN_CIDR,
#   - preflights that this box actually HOLDS LAGUNA_LAN_IP and that it sits inside
#     LAGUNA_LAN_CIDR — aborting with a clear message if the config and reality disagree.
#
# Caddy's internal CA issues the cert with LAGUNA_LAN_IP in the SAN, so changing the IP
# here (and re-running install.ps1) re-issues the cert automatically.
#
# env\box.env is gitignored (it's per-box). Keep this .example committed.

# The static/DHCP-reserved IPv4 address this box serves on. Pin it with a router DHCP
# reservation (bind the box MAC -> this IP) so it never changes. Tablets browse to
# https://<LAGUNA_LAN_IP>.
LAGUNA_LAN_IP=192.168.0.123

# The LAN subnet allowed to reach the box, in CIDR form. Used BOTH for the firewall
# rule and the Caddyfile @notlan app-layer guard. LAGUNA_LAN_IP must fall inside it.
# Tighten to specific tablet IPs (comma-separated) later if you want.
LAGUNA_LAN_CIDR=192.168.0.0/24

# OPTIONAL: the local DNS hostname this box serves on (e.g. pos.laguna.lan). When set,
# Caddy serves BOTH this name and LAGUNA_LAN_IP, and the internal-CA cert carries both in
# its SAN — so tablets can browse https://<host> or https://<LAGUNA_LAN_IP>. Requires a
# matching local DNS record (host -> LAGUNA_LAN_IP) on the router. Comma-separate to serve
# several names. Leave BLANK for an IP-only box (no DNS needed) — nothing else changes.
LAGUNA_LAN_HOST=pos.laguna.lan
